A||N||11||bHooper||different flywheels||||5||
Z||000000||bHooper||04-11-2000||08:51 PM||||when i bought my new tranny (from a 87 corsica with a getrag) i took the flywheel.  i did this because it was cheaper than having my old one turned (to eliminate the rust).<P>however, upon closer inspection, they appear slightly different.  the GT flywheel has a built in counter weight on the back, that is not on the corsica one.<P>will this cause me any problems?  can i use the corsica flywheel?  or should i get the old one turned and be happy with that?<P>thanks,<BR>hoop||||reg||5
Z||000001||Rodney||04-11-2000||09:51 PM||||Get the original one ground at a place that does them. No other choice here. <P>Rodney Dickman||||reg||1
Z||000002||bHooper||04-11-2000||10:30 PM||||thanks, Rodney.  will do!<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000003||WKDFIRO||04-11-2000||11:01 PM||||In 88 they balanced the crankshaft so the flywheel had no counterweight. In all the others it did. We had a car that had a real bad shudder just vibrating throughout the car the Fiero was an 88. Guess what year flywheel they put on? One with a counterweight. If you had an 88 the Corsica flywheel would be no problem. Better to stick with what you got. Very interested to see how well the tranny works though! I didn't know Corsicas came with manual trannys!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phil||04-12-2000||08:23 AM||||Be sure to hang onto that flywheel. Anybody that is doing a 3.1 conversion is going to need one(manual of course).||||reg||1
Z||000005||bHooper||04-12-2000||08:54 AM||||so the 2.8 l corsica flywheel would be fine for anyone upgrading to a 3.1 l?  but not for my 2.8 in the GT... interesting.<P>there is much to learn in life, isnt there?||||reg||1
Z||000006||lowCG||04-12-2000||09:23 AM||||Has anyone ever lightened a flywheel?<BR>Was going to try this on my early flywheel,have it nuetral balanced at the same time for use on a 3.4.||||reg||1
Z||000007||HarryG||04-12-2000||12:36 PM||||low CG:<BR>IMHO...For a street application, any gain in acceleration from lightening the flywheel would be more than offset by loss of drivability. Besides, if you've ever seen the damage a flywheel/clutch can do when it comes apart... and since the driver's seat is about in the same plane as the flywheel, I wouldn't even consider lightening a stock one!!  ||||reg||13
Z||000008||lowCG||04-12-2000||08:11 PM||||Well my name isn't John Force,just using a basically stock clutch,which doesn't do a whole lot when it comes apart,and if I wanted a really light flywheel,I'd go look for an aluminum one anyways.<BR>What I was asking was if someone had removed the balance weight portion of the flywheel,since I would much rather have a seasoned,tested flywheel over a new one,and verify the balance at the same time.||||reg||1
Z||000009||batboy||04-12-2000||10:33 PM||||Low, a machine shop should be able to remove the weight and rebalance it. Chevy 454 engines are externally balanced and have weights on the flywheel/flex plates and on the front damper too, but they can be redone to work on internally balanced big blocks (396, 427, etc.) or vice versa, weights can be added to internally balanced parts to make them useable for the 454.<P>BHooper, the 3.1 and 3.4 (and the 88 Fiero 2.8) all use the same flywheel/flexplate. The Corsica must of been a 3.1 or else it's an internally balanced 2.8 like the 88 Fiero. I didn't know they ever put the Getrag in Corsicas. I thought it was only on the performance cars, like the Z-24 or other limited edition cars.||||reg||1
Z||000010||bHooper||04-13-2000||09:04 AM||||allow me to quote Rodney's web site for the info regarding donar cars:<P>"Since so few Fiero specific Getrags were made, and since obviously many are<BR>still in operation, you do have an option: Obtain a 5-speed Getrag from a<BR>non-Fiero car and transplant it into your Fiero! A general list of cars that used<BR>this transmission on a 2.8 or 3.1 are as follows:<P>Cavalier 87-89<BR>6000 87-88<BR>Beretta 87-89<P>Celebrity 87-88<BR>Corsica 87-89<BR>Cimarron 87-88<P>Fierenza 87"<P>However you may actually be on to something, about the Corsica flywheel!  While I do not believe it to be a 3.1 liter car, it does make a lot of sense that the flywheel is balanced.  Unlike the one on my 86.5 GT.  It could also be possible that GM balanced the flywheel in 87, not 88 like had been mentioned earlier.<P>Either way, I will take the safer route (for now) and have my old flywheel turned so that I can get this darned car back together in the next few weeks....<P>Afterall, I have a bet to win!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||lowCG||04-13-2000||12:48 PM||||So it's an actual chunk of metal,not just holes drilled on the opposite side?<BR>Yet another hurdle in the swap zone.<BR>Have been holding off on separating the old engine and trans until I get back from  vacation,and can get the new clutch,motor mounts to do it all at once,so I haven't seen the flywheel yet.||||reg||1
